  "title": "First Customer Onboarding Risk Review",
  "rationale": "The three most important risks to address before onboarding the first paying customer are: 1. Customer control, consent, and spending risk: KoreLumina must ensure the customer retains control of code, data, keys, infrastructure, deployment path, and AI-provider choice, and that no costly operation runs without a customer-approved execution plan covering estimated credits, cost, time, models, infrastructure impact, artifacts, and rollback. 2. Production execution, validation, and recovery risk: because KoreLumina sells a workspace that imports, runs, analyzes, repairs, previews, and deploys customer software, first-customer readiness depends on grounded runtime truth, validation gates, observability before automation, manual recovery paths, clear rollback points, and build/runtime checks before changes affect customer work. 3. Governance, trust, and capability-claim risk: KoreLumina must not trade governance, validation, knowledge preservation, or human approval for speed or revenue; the Chief Agent must not bypass high-impact approval gates, fabricate runtime state, ignore repository governance, introduce undocumented architecture, or make unsupported customer-facing claims such as undeclared certifications. These are the highest-priority risks because they directly affect customer trust, customer assets, paid execution safety, and the governed AI-operated engineering model.",
  "requestedBy": "chief-agent",
  "status": "proposed",
